.\" man.1 -- maux's man(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An original
.\" page (this project's own text), so it carries no third-party
.\" notice -- MIT like the code, same posture as env.1. Rendered
.\" at build time to build/man/MAN.TXT and staged on the play
.\" image as /usr/man/MAN: `more /usr/man/MAN`.
.TH MAN 1 "September 15, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
man \- display a manual page
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B man
[
.I section
]
.I name
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Man
locates the manual page for
.I name
at
.BR /usr/man/ NAME
(the name upper\-cased -- every page in this tree is staged
there in upper case) and writes it to standard output.  A
leading numeric
.I section
operand is accepted and ignored: this tree ships exactly one
section.
.PP
If standard output is a terminal,
.I man
pages the file through
.BR more (1)
(exiting with
.I more's
own status).  Otherwise \(em standard output redirected to a
file or a pipe \(em the page is copied through unpaged, the
same way
.I more
itself behaves when its own output is not a console.
.PP
.B \-k
and
.B \-f
(apropos/whatis) are not implemented: this tree builds no page
index to search.
.SH FILES
.br
/usr/man/NAME  every page, including this one
.SH "SEE ALSO"
more(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0; 1 if no page named
.I name
exists, or the pager could not be run; 2 for a usage error
(wrong operand count, a non\-numeric section, or an operand
beginning with
.BR \- ).
.SH BUGS
No apropos/whatis index (see above). A page name longer than 20
characters cannot exist on this tree's volumes and is reported
as "no manual entry" rather than a separate diagnostic.
